course: dictionary
lesson: 13053
title: Fattening: food and farms
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: «Fattening» has two meanings, and the sentence tells you which one to use. As an adjective it describes food that makes you put on weight. As a verb, it describes making an animal or a person fatter. Both come from the word fat.
explain: Read the words around it. «Chips and sweets are fattening, so have them only now and then.» The clue is «only now and then», so fattening must mean food that can make you put on weight. Check by swapping in your meaning: does it still make sense?
explain: «The farmer is fattening the pigs before they go to market.» Here fattening is a verb. The farmer is making the pigs fatter so they can be sold. Fattening up is a phrase you may also hear, meaning feeding someone or an animal until they get bigger.
fact: As an adjective, fattening describes food, as in «fattening food».
fact: «Fattening» means likely to make you put on weight, usually because it has lots of fat or sugar.
fact: As a verb, «fatten» means making an animal or person fatter, especially farm animals before they are sold.
fact: Chips, sweets and fizzy drinks are fattening, so they are fine sometimes but should not be eaten every day.
exercise: «Sweets are fattening, so eat them only now and then.» What does fattening mean here? | Very tasty and crunchy | Cold and frozen | Good for your teeth | *Likely to make you put on weight
exercise: «The farmer is fattening the geese before the market.» What is the farmer doing? | Letting the geese fly free in the field | Cleaning the geese's pond | *Making the geese fatter so they can be sold | Counting the geese every morning
exercise: Fizzy drinks are a fattening treat. How often should you have them? | Every day at breakfast | *Only sometimes, not every day | Never, not even once in your life | Every time you feel thirsty
test: In the story of «Hansel and Gretel», the witch was «fattening Hansel». What was she doing to Hansel? | Making him thinner | Making him run faster | *Making him fatter | Teaching him to cook
test: Which sentence uses fattening as a verb? | *The farmer is fattening up the cows for market. | Sam likes fattening foods at lunch. | Tom ate a bowl of fattening crisps. | The fattening cake was very sweet.
exam: Ella says, «Fattening food is always bad, so never eat it at all.» Is she right? | *No, fattening food is fine sometimes but should not be eaten every day | No, because fattening means very thin | Yes, never eat any sweets or chips at all | Yes, because fattening food is always cold
